// PLAYTEST: is a Striker's damage routine worth the round it costs, and
// does it read as *this class's* move rather than a bigger swing?
//
// One of seven files — `player-class-striker`, `-bastion`, `-medic`,
// `-saboteur`, `-leech`, `-invoker`, `-decompiler` — that are **identical
// in every field but `character`**. The fight, the party, the level, the
// zone and the seed are the same in all seven, so whatever separates them
// is the class and nothing else. That is the whole claim the class design makes, and these files are
// here to fail it.
//
//     FERAL_DEV_ARENA=1 cargo run  ->  [R] Arena  ->  [L] player-class-striker
//
// **The headless bin cannot see a class, and will report the same numbers
// for all seven.** That is not an ignored input; it is the seam. `run_rep`
// plays `PartyPlan::AllAttack` — the game's own `[A]` — which invokes no
// routine, and a class is a spread of multipliers over *authored routine
// power* alone. The player's ordinary swing never touches
// `Game::ability_affinity` at all; it is `attack_range` into
// `resolve_attack`. So the bin is the **floor**: what this party does with
// no class and no routines. What you play is the class, measured against it.
//
//     cargo run --bin arena -- dev-arenas/player-class-striker.ron
//
// **The floor, measured 2026-09-01 at 20 reps, seeds 1-20** (identical for
// all seven files, and for a `character: ()` control):
//
//     win rate        60%   (12/20)
//     rounds          mean 18.6, median 17
//     player HP left  mean 26%
//     companions down mean 1.15
//
// Compare deltas within one build only — a moved baseline across reports is
// a reshuffled RNG stream, not a difficulty change. Re-run the floor from
// the same build before reading anything off a played fight.
//
// The pack is chosen for **rounds to spend**, `class-mirror.ron`'s argument:
// a routine cannot prove anything in a fight that ends before it is worth
// invoking. At zone 1 with a level-5 party, five sub_processes in each of
// two groups is a 19-round fight won at a fifth Integrity — damage worth
// undoing, and the time to undo it in. Two groups so the front/back rank
// rules apply. Power is not the constraint: `POWER_MAX` is a flat 100 at
// every level and this routine costs 8, so the invocation budget is not what
// the fight is testing.
//
// Seed 2 is a 19-round win at 22% Integrity remaining — the shape described
// above. `[N]` steps to the next seed; 5, 9 and 12 are 20, 17 and 19 rounds
// and also wins, 3 and 10 are losses.
//
// The stat pool is left unspent (`stats: (0, 0, 0, 0)`) deliberately: the
// pool *is* visible to the bin, so spending it here would move the floor and
// mix the two questions. `player-points-atk.ron` and
// `player-points-integrity.ron` are where the pool is asked about.
//
// What to watch:
//   - Fork Bomb Single at 1.3 against the same routine at 0.8 on a Medic:
//     is a 60% swing in authored power something you can feel across a
//     fight, or is it lost in the band spread?
//   - Striker damps Heal. There is no heal in this kit to damp, so a
//     Striker's trade is invisible here by construction — which is itself
//     the finding if the class reads as strictly better than the others.
//   - Does spending a round on the routine beat spending it attacking? If
//     it never does, the class arrives as a dead row and the whole spread
//     is unreachable.
(
    player: Fresh(level: 5, zone: 1),
    character: (
        class: Some(Striker),
        stats: (0, 0, 0, 0),
        routine: Some("stack_smash"),
    ),
    party: [
        (species: "glitch", level: 5),
        (species: "glitch", level: 5),
    ],
    opponents: [
        (species: "sub_process", count: 5),
        (species: "sub_process", count: 5),
    ],
    reps: 1,
    seed: 2,
)
